1)The Government of Azerbaijan requested technical assistance from the United Nations (UN), European Union (EU), and the World Bank Group to support its efforts for a post-conflict recovery needs assessment in its liberated territories. WHO Azerbaijan was leading the Pillar II of Health Sector of JRNA and jointly worked on data analysis, main needs and priorities. On the final stage WHO wanted to communicate the findings to the Health Sector UN representatives (UNICEF, UNHCR) and National Health Sector Stakeholders (MFA, MoH, SAMHI, TABIB, State Statistical Committee, Organization for Migration) and organized the workshop to discuss the findings and recommendations. 
The needs and priorities of JRNA were finalized in consultation with national stakeholders before being submitted to the Government. The short-, mid-, and long-term priorities, determined by implementation mechanisms, were categorized in alignment with the views of national health sector stakeholders. 

2)"Coordination and Transformation of Mental Health Services in the Post-Conflict settings" project

The WHO Country Office in Azerbaijan implemented the Coordination and Transformation of Mental Health Services in the Post-Conflict settings project with the financial support of the EU-ECHO funding (March 2022 - September 2023).
The main objective of the project is to reduce mental health and psychosocial suffering, improve the well-being of conflict-affected populations and improve their access to Mental Health and Psychosocial Support (MHPSS) services. The project aims to improve existing MHPSS coordination structures and create sustainable networking and capacity building between relevant governmental and non-governmental organizations. The project is also designed to address post-conflict needs and offer long-term resilience opportunities. The project covers 5 districts close to the contact line: Aghdam, Agjabedi, Barda, Fuzuli and Tartar.
Achievements achieved within the framework of the project:
•	  A national training team for mhGAP consisting of 20 psychiatrists and primary health care physicians was established;
•	  Supervisions by an international expert on mhGAP were organized for national trainers;
•	  11 trainings and continuous supervisions were organized in the target regions and more than 170 primary health care physicians' knowledge and skills related to mental health were increased;
•	  Teams consisting of psychologists and social workers were created to provide psychosocial support in the target regions;
•	  6 educational campaigns related to mental health and psychosocial support were organized in the target regions, and services were provided to about 5,000 citizens within the framework of these campaigns;
•	  The project team introduced the project at the "EuroVillage " event of the European Union in Lankaran and provided information about mental health and psychosocial support, including stigma related to mental illnesses;
•	  A prefabricated office for a psychologist and a social worker has been installed in Aghdam Inter-Regional Psychiatric Dispensary;
•	  Training on "Basic principles of psychosocial support" and then supervisions were organized for about 50 coordinators of TABIB on work with vulnerable population groups;
•	  Training for Trainers on "Mental health and psychosocial support" was held for about 20 psychologists and doctors in order to train other health workers of TABIB;
•	  A Coordinating group has been established, representing relevant stakeholders (state and international organizations) on mental health and psychosocial support.

